\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 Throughout this passage and throughout wider American literature, the conflict between traditional lifestyles and modernity is vital and frequently explored. This is shown in other novels I have studied, such as The Grapes of Wrath. In The Grapes of Wrath, the desire for a traditional agrarian lifestyle is represented by characters such as Ma Joad, whereas characters such as Connie and Al Joad desire modernity. Similarly, in this passage Henry desires modernity and change, whereas his mother seeks to maintain the life they have always had. Henry views war as distinctly European ('heavy crowns and high castles') and something of the past. In this way Henry seeks to form a new identity for himself using neoclassical ideas to make himself into a 'great man', which can be seen through his mention of the battles being 'Homeric' or 'Greeklike'. This can relate to another novel I read, The Great Gatsby, where similarly Gatsby leaves his family in search of greatness in the form of wealth and success. This ambitious abandonment of modern life is consistent throughout American literature and can correlate to the American Dream. The American Dream is quite individualised, and Henry's dreams ('dreamed' 'visions') are based on war. American identity being linked to war is a commonplace idea. The national holiday of America celebrates battle victory, and veteran culture is incredibly important to this day, whilst also having the most powerful military in Henry's time. Thus, it only makes sense that when trying to forge a new modern life and identity, Henry looks to war and conflict, something he uniquely views as in the past. Despite seeing war as the past, Henry sees it as his future and his path into a modern life away from his family's traditions. \par
\par
 \par
\par
This passage appears to be taken from a Bildungsroman and begins with a flashback. This is indicated by the first paragraph where it is apparent that the narrator is reflecting on the past before they enlisted. The narrator at first appears to be omniscient and reliable, but the use of the word \ldblquote perhaps\rdblquote  in \ldblquote On the morrow, perhaps\rdblquote  is significant. The author is writing this in 1895, which is three decades after the end of the civil war, and this choice to make the narrator grounded in their time is conscious and works to portray this as the retrospective account of a character. The use of language greatly affects how a reader is likely to interpret the authenticity of the events described in the passage. The use of the words \ldblquote dreams\rdblquote  and \ldblquote visions\rdblquote  in the next paragraph is highly significant. The young character Henry in this passage glorifies and idealises war, which could be considered reflective of how American culture pushes young men into service. It also further adds to the youth and naivete of Henry, as to him, such a serious thing as a conflict or war is a \ldblquote dream\rdblquote  and something heroic. Further, I found the use of the phrase \ldblquote eagle-eyed\rdblquote  notable, as the eagle is frequently used as a patriotic symbol for America. Due to the language used about conflict in this paragraph, Henry, to the reader, seems to be the average patriotic young American man, an everyman. The phrase \ldblquote heavy crowns and high castles\rdblquote  is distinctly European and places war somewhat outside of the typical American experience. The author of the passage could be using this language to convey the strangeness of conflict happening directly on American soil. Additionally, it further adds to the idea of Henry\rquote s naivety and need to explore the world more. The long multiclausal sentence at the end of this paragraph works to convey how long Henry thinks war is in the past. The reader is forced to endure this long passage of time correlating to the passage of time Henry thinks has passed since the \ldblquote time of wars\rdblquote . Additionally, the use of the phrase \ldblquote over the horizon\rdblquote  conjures images of the sun, which always returns despite setting. War is indirectly compared to something everlasting (from a human perspective) and natural. The use of the word \ldblquote youthful\rdblquote  at the start of the next paragraph further reinforces the use of the word \ldblquote youth\rdblquote  at the beginning and directs the reader to further understand Henry as someone who has not come of age. He describes war in his country as a \ldblquote play affair\rdblquote , and this further reinforces the idea that culturally war was considered much more of a European thing and not a part of 19th-century American society. The paragraph continues to further Henry\rquote s idea that war is something European and of the past. It is described as \ldblquote Greeklike\rdblquote  and that \ldblquote education had effaced the throat-grappling instinct\rdblquote . This further distances American culture from European, and perhaps the use of language in this passage shows the emergence of a distinct cultural identity emerging through American literature at this time. In this way America and its culture act like Henry throughout this passage, wishing to go its own way but relying on European ideals to some extent. The author seems to invite this comparison through their use of language. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The next section of this passage begins with a simple sentence which further shows the immediacy and intensity of Henry\rquote s wish to enlist and fight. The further use of another simple sentence talking about \ldblquote great movements\rdblquote  furthers the intensity of the conflict. It seems as if the conflict is massive and indescribable and thus can only be referred to in simple terms, as words would not do the true scale of it justice. The naivety of Henry\rquote s youth is repeatedly emphasised throughout the passage, including further by the use of the word \ldblquote glory\rdblquote  to describe conflict. This shows his total inexperience of war. The writer's choice to repeatedly reinforce this point is both notable as a way of showing Henry\rquote s character and his flaws and to criticise war in general. War has sucked up the naive and impressionable young Henry, and the repetition of this conveys a strong view. The use of the word \ldblquote lurid\rdblquote  is an important choice by the author, as this conveys the intense sensory experience Henry is imagining of war. Henry, who is inexperienced in war and in life in general, is clearly imagining the \ldblquote extravagant\rdblquote  \ldblquote colour\rdblquote , which sharply contrasts the actual uniforms of the period, which were dull and dark or tan. It is made clear that Henry\rquote s imagination gets away from the actual reality of the event he wishes to engage in. The next paragraph begins with another simple sentence which sharply shows his mother\rquote s disapproval of his decision to try and enlist. This again is used to emphasise a character's intentions and beliefs, such as was done for Henry in the last paragraph. This creates an almost mirror-like structure which could either show the matching strength of each character's view or show the intrinsic bond between mother and child. Her described doubt of his \ldblquote ardour\rdblquote  and \ldblquote patriotism\rdblquote  must be understood to be fair by the reader by this point. It has been repeatedly shown that Henry is both naive and knows little of war, and his motivations are shown to be \ldblquote glory\rdblquote  and not a patriotism or ideological drive. The reader must regard it as strange that Henry does not mention political motivations in a war as politically and morally charged as the American Civil War. This further works to question Henry\rquote s knowledge of the real world and the legitimacy of his desire to go to war. The use of the words \ldblquote hundreds\rdblquote  and \ldblquote vastly\rdblquote  in the next sentence quickly convey that not only is she more knowledgeable but also better at arguing, showing the gulf in her and her son\rquote s experience. Additionally, this paragraph features multiple longer multiclausal sentences showing the reader the further development and rationality of Henry\rquote s mother's arguments and reasons for him not enlisting. Additionally, it is stated she has a \ldblquote deep conviction\rdblquote  and \ldblquote ethical\rdblquote  reasons which strongly and intentionally contrast Henry\rquote s desire for adventure and glory that could be found through conflict and battle. This paragraph uses structure and language to show the irrationality of Henry\rquote s reasoning and his youth in comparison to his mother. It is notable that the next paragraph, where Henry decides to enlist and thus argues back against his mother, is much shorter, showing further the lack of rationality and good argument from Henry. The use of \ldblquote yellow light\rdblquote  invites the reader to bring illusions to the common imagery of yellow being associated with cowardice. Henry decides to make \ldblquote firm rebellion\rdblquote  against his mother. This could show bravery or foolishness. It also could be considered a metaphorical representation of the South in the American Civil War, who decided to try and secede from the Union and firmly rebel.   \par
\par
 \par
\par
In the final section of the passage, Henry finally enlists, much to the dismay of his mother. In the first sentence the phrase \ldblquote twisted news\rdblquote  is used to describe the reports of a \ldblquote great battle\rdblquote . This conveys a displeasure with war either from the narrator or from the perspective of the author inserting their beliefs. The word \ldblquote twisted\rdblquote  for the reader could conjure images of the damaged and mutilated corpses of many soldiers who had died in the \ldblquote great battle\rdblquote . The only use of speech from characters in this passage humanises the characters and names them. Until this point we had only had the characters motivations described and had not actually heard from them. Henry declaring to his mum, \ldblquote Ma, I\rquote m going to enlist,\rdblquote  without question shows his intentions and that he has already decided. For a reader, there is a finality in the definitive statement made from Henry here, and it seems his mind is already. Additionally, his mother's decision to not directly tell him not to must be considered notable. The reader interprets that his mother realises that Henry has already made his decision. By naming Henry here, he is humanised and personalised, previously only being referred to by the use of pronouns. At this point for the reader, he is no longer an anonymised soldier but someone who will potentially go to a war for a superficial reason and die. This decision holds a large amount of emotional weight for the reader. Additionally, it could be considered his mother directly addressing him showing a fear he may not return, so there is an attempt to try and reconnect their relationship by being more affectionate. In the next paragraph he informs his mother he has enlisted and comes back to her performing farm work. This shows the constant work that his mother described in the \ldblquote hundreds\rdblquote  of reasons he was needed; the \ldblquote four\rdblquote  standing by also give the reader the impression that the farm needs all the help it can get. Additionally, it is notable that the author describes Henry as doing this \ldblquote diffidently\rdblquote , showing a lack of confidence in the correctness of his decision. Henry\rquote s youth is further shown in that even when he tries to be defiant, he is unsure and unconfident. The repetition of Henry\rquote s name by his mother and the invocation of the \ldblquote Lord\rdblquote  show the seriousness and fear his mother is feeling due to his decision to enlist. Religion held a muchmore important role at this point and shows a seriousness from his mother. This idea is reinforced by the \ldblquote tears\rdblquote  mentioned in the last sentence of the passage. These \ldblquote tears\rdblquote  strongly juxtapose the \ldblquote excitement\rdblquote  in Henry\rquote s eyes. This shows the emotional gulf between Henry and his mother and displays his youthful innocence to the horrors of war. The \ldblquote youth\rdblquote  of Henry that started the passage strongly contrasts with his mother\rquote s \ldblquote scarred cheeks\rdblquote , and this continues the mirror-like contrasting structure between him and his mother. \par
